const e={slug:"agrippa",title:"Agrippa and the Three Books",otherNames:["De occulta philosophia libri tres, the Latin title of 1533","Three Books of Occult Philosophy, J.F.’s English of 1651","“Three Books of Occult Philosophy, or of Magick”, the head-title of Book I in 1651","Henry Cornelius Agrippa, as the English editions name him"],family:"Not an alphabet: the author and the source book — the Renaissance compendium of natural, celestial and ceremonial magic that gathered the Theban, the three Hebrew-keyed alphabets, the nine chambers and the tables of the planets into one handbook.",keyedTo:"none",direction:"ltr",status:"ready",summary:"Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim (1486–1535) wrote De occulta philosophia as a young man and sent the draft to Abbot Trithemius, who replied on 8 April 1510. He printed Book I alone in 1531 and the three books, much enlarged, in July 1533, though in 1530 he had retracted the work in a declamation on the vanity of the sciences. Five of this wing’s alphabets stand in Book III, chapters 29 and 30. The standard English is J.F.’s of 1651.",intro:["Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim wrote the first version of De occulta philosophia as a young man, after a stay with Johannes Trithemius at the abbey of St Jakob in Würzburg, and sent it to the abbot for his “correction and censure”. Trithemius answered on 8 April 1510 with praise and a rule: common secrets for common friends, the higher ones for higher friends only. By Agrippa’s own account the draft then went from hand to hand in Italy, France and Germany, and more than twenty years passed before he printed it: Book I alone in 1531, and the three books, much enlarged, in July 1533.","The book is built on a threefold world. Book I treats natural magic, the virtues of the elemental world; Book II celestial magic, number, figure and the stars; Book III ceremonial magic, the intellectual world of religion, the divine names and the spirits. Characters climb the same ladder: the seals the stars stamp on stones, plants and the human hand (I.33); celestial characters, made from the geomantic figures and by likeness (II.51–52); and, in Book III, the characters and seals of spirits (III.29) and the scripts “delivered by Cabalists” (III.30). That is where the wing’s Agrippan alphabets stand — the Honorian alphabet, later called Theban, in III.29; the Celestial writing, the Malachim, the Passing of the River and the nine chambers in III.30.","Agrippa claims none of them as his own. He gives the Honorian alphabet on Peter of Abano’s report of Honorius of Thebes, the three Hebrew-keyed scripts to “the Hebrews” and the nine chambers to the Cabalists, and two of them were in print before him: the Honorian in Trithemius’s Polygraphia of 1518, and the Passing of the River, as reported, in a Hebrew grammar printed at Venice in 1523. What his book did was gather them, give them a place in a theory of signs, and pass them on. Through J.F.’s English of 1651 and the plate in Francis Barrett’s Magus of 1801 they reached later readers; that most modern charts copy these versions is often said, but has not been shown.","The book carries its own retraction. In 1530, a year before Book I appeared, Agrippa published a declamation on the uncertainty and vanity of the sciences which attacks magic with every other art and recants De occulta philosophia; the edition of 1533 prints that recantation at its end, before the colophon. Whether he meant it is still argued. The “Fourth Book” printed under his name after his death is not his: Johann Weyer, who had served in his household as his famulus, rejected it, and scholars treat it as spurious."],firstAttestation:{work:"De occulta philosophia, the first version, sent in manuscript to Johannes Trithemius",author:"Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim (1486–1535; the catalogue heading queries the year of birth)",date:"8 April 1510 — the latest date for the draft: the day of Trithemius’s reply, “the 8. day of April, An. M. D. X.” in the English of 1651, “VIII. die Aprilis” in the Latin of 1533",year:1510,place:"Written for Trithemius after Agrippa’s stay at his abbey of St Jakob at Würzburg — “your Monastery of Herbipolis”, as the English of 1651 has it — a visit the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy dates to the winter of 1509–10. Trithemius replied from the same house, which he calls Peapolis.",locator:"The front matter of the 1533 edition and of the English of 1651: Agrippa’s letter to Trithemius, which carries no date in the 1651 text, and Trithemius’s reply, both printed before Book I.",notes:"The text of 1510 is not the printed one, and Agrippa says so himself. In 1527 he called the first two books “dificient in many things” and the third “wholy imperfect”; in the dedication of January 1531 he says he has “added many things to many Chapters”. The early redaction is reported in Vittoria Perrone Compagni’s critical edition (Leiden, 1992). Until a chapter is shown to stand in the draft, no chapter of Books II and III, the alphabets included, can be dated before the printing of 1533."},lettersTitle:"The Chapters",valueLabel:"Where it stands",scriptNote:"Not an alphabet: the twenty-three chapters of the three books that matter most to this wing, with the front matter before them and the Censure after. In all, Book I has seventy-four chapters, Book II sixty and Book III sixty-five. Each is cited by book and chapter as in the Latin of 1533 and in Perrone Compagni’s edition, which keep the order of the English volume; the headings, the pages and the words quoted are J.F.’s English of 1651. The book’s own figures — the alphabets, squares, seals and signs — are not reproduced here: each has its own page in the wing.",letters:[{order:1,name:"Front matter",gloss:"“To the Reader”, the letters of 1510, and the dedication of Book I",value:"Before Book I, in the Latin of 1533 and the English of 1651 (unpaginated)",numeric:null,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Four documents stand before Book I: “To the Reader”, Agrippa’s letter to Trithemius, Trithemius’s reply and the dedication of Book I. The reply is dated “From our Monastery of Peapolis, the 8. day of April, An. M. D. X.”; the dedication of Book I, to Hermann von Wied, archbishop of Cologne, is dated “From Mechlinia, Anno M. D. XXXI. In the moneth of January”, that is, Mechelen in January 1531. In “To the Reader” Agrippa says that copies of the draft flew abroad “through many mens hands” and that he had “for the most part” retracted the book. J.F.’s “Neapolitanian Abbot” is a slip: the Latin of 1533 has “abbati Peapolitano”."},{order:2,name:"I.1 · The three worlds",gloss:"How Magicians Collect vertues from the three-fold World, is declared in these three Books",value:"Book I, chapter 1; pp. 1–2 in the English of 1651",numeric:1,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Agrippa’s plan of the whole work: a world in three, “Elementary, Celestiall, and intellectuall”, each part governed by the one above it, and three books to match. Virtues come down from God through the angels, the heavens, the stars and the elements, and the wise, he says, think it possible “to ascend by the same degrees through each World”."},{order:3,name:"I.33 · Seals of natural things",gloss:"Of the Seals, and Characters of Naturall things",value:"Book I, chapter 33; pp. 65–68 in the English of 1651",numeric:33,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The lowest rung of Agrippa’s theory of characters: every star stamps its seal or character on the stones, plants and animals beneath it, and on the lines of the human hand. The chapter ends with seven small figures, “The Letters, or Characters” of Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the Sun, Venus, Mercury and the Moon (p. 68 in 1651), which it calls divine letters."},{order:4,name:"I.70 · Proper names",gloss:"Of the vertue of proper names",value:"Book I, chapter 70; pp. 153–154 in the English of 1651",numeric:70,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The power of names, grounded in Adam’s naming of the creatures. “Magicians say”, Agrippa reports, that “proper names of things are certain rayes of things”, which keep the power of what they name. It belongs to a run of chapters on the spoken word (I.69–72) that leads to writing in I.73 and to letters in I.74. The story of Apollonius raising a girl once he had learned her name is reported from Philostratus."},{order:5,name:"I.73 · Writing",gloss:"Of the vertue of writting, and of making imprecations, and inscriptions",value:"Book I, chapter 73; pp. 159–160 in the English of 1651",numeric:73,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The hinge from speech to letters: writing is “the last expression of the mind”, which fixes and keeps what the act of the voice cannot. Agrippa adds that “Magicians command, that in every work, there be imprecations, and inscriptions made”, citing Albertus’s Speculum and Virgil’s eighth Eclogue. The chapter has no figures."},{order:6,name:"I.74 · Letters and the heavens",gloss:"Of the proportion, correspondency, reduction of Letters to the Celestiall Signs, and Planets, according to various tongues, with a Table shewing this",value:"Book I, chapter 74; pp. 160–163 in the English of 1651, with a folding table",numeric:74,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The chapter the alphabets of Book III rest on. The twenty-two Hebrew letters are “the foundation of the world”, divided into twelve simple, seven double and three mothers for the signs, the planets and the elements — the scheme of the Sefer Yetzirah, though Agrippa does not name the book. His mothers are fire, water and earth, for “they account Aire no Element”; the Hebrew hall gives the Sefer Yetzirah’s air, water and fire, and the two are left as each gives them. Greek and Latin are fitted to the same frame: in Latin the five vowels with consonantal J and V answer the seven planets, twelve consonants the signs, K, Q, X and Z the elements, and H the spirit of the world. “The same rule is amongst the Chaldeans”, the chapter adds, and II.19 says that the Chaldean alphabet is set down “in the end of the first book”, where this chapter’s table stands. The table is not reproduced here."},{order:7,name:"II.19 · Signs for numbers",gloss:"Of the notes of the Hebrews, and Caldeans, and certain other notes of Magicians",value:"Book II, chapter 19; pp. 232–234 in the English of 1651",numeric:19,glyph:null,formConfidence:"medium",note:"Two kinds of number-sign. First the Hebrew letters as numerals, the five finals counting 500 to 900, and fifteen written as nine and six, not ten and five, “out of honor to the divine name”. Then “certain most elegant marks of numbers” that Agrippa says he found in “two most ancient books of Astrologers, and Magicians”: signs for one to nine, turned to the left hand for the tens, and turned downwards for the hundreds on the right hand and the thousands on the left. That rule is the rule of the medieval number-signs now called the ciphers of the monks."},{order:8,name:"II.20 · The numbers of letters",gloss:"What numbers are attributed to letters; and of divining by the same",value:"Book II, chapter 20; pp. 235–237 in the English of 1651",numeric:20,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Agrippa’s numbers for the Latin alphabet: A to I count 1 to 9, K to S 10 to 90, T, V, X, Y and Z 100 to 500, then consonantal J 600, consonantal V 700, HI 800 and HV 900. The Pythagoreans, he says, read the future from such numbers, “Whence they call this kind of divination Arithmancy”. The values are his scheme, not Roman practice."},{order:9,name:"II.22 · Tables of the planets",gloss:"Of the tables of the Planets, their vertues, forms, and what Divine names, Intelligencies, and Spirits are set over them",value:"Book II, chapter 22; pp. 239–252 in the English of 1651",numeric:22,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The seven magic squares, Saturn’s of three by three to the Moon’s of nine by nine, each printed in figures and again “In Hebrew notes”, with the divine names, the Intelligence and the Spirit of each planet and their seals. How the seals are drawn from the squares Agrippa leaves to “the wise searcher”. In the English of 1651 several numbers in the squares of Venus, Mercury and the Moon are misprinted, and its errata correct some of them."},{order:10,name:"II.48 · Geomantic figures",gloss:"Of Geomanticall Figures, which are the middle betwixt Images and Characters",value:"Book II, chapter 48; pp. 309–310 in the English of 1651",numeric:48,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The sixteen figures of geomancy, into which diviners reduce the points of their lot, set out in a table with their names, elements, planets and signs. Engraved under the dominion of their planets, Agrippa says, they take on the power of images, and so they stand “as a middle betwixt Images and Characters”."},{order:11,name:"II.51 · Celestial characters",gloss:"Of Characters which are made after the rule and imitation of Celestial, and how with the table thereof they are deduced out of Geomantical figures",value:"Book II, chapter 51; pp. 316–319 in the English of 1651",numeric:51,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The middle rung of the theory: characters for the planets made by binding together the points of their geomantic figures, two to each planet, with the Dragon’s Head and Tail, in a table. Here the writing Malachim is first mentioned, as “the writing of Angels”, and set aside with “But of these hereafter” — a pointer to III.30, where it is printed."},{order:12,name:"II.52 · Characters by likeness",gloss:"Of Characters which are drawn from things themselves by a certain likeness",value:"Book II, chapter 52; pp. 320–323 in the English of 1651",numeric:52,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Characters drawn from things by likeness, which Agrippa calls “images ill dearticulated”: Aries and Taurus from their horns, Libra from a balance, Saturn from a sickle, Venus from a looking-glass, the Moon from her horns. Then the characters of the triplicities and of conjunctions, and fifteen “which Hermes assigned to the fixed stars, and Behenii”. The derivations are his explanation of the signs, not their history, which the Signs of the Art gives."},{order:13,name:"III.10 · The ten sefirot",gloss:"Of Divine emanations, which the Hebrews call Numerations, others attributes: The gentiles gods and Dieties; and of the ten Sephiroths and ten most sacred names of God which rule them, and the interpretation of them",value:"Book III, chapter 10; pp. 366–370 in the English of 1651",numeric:10,glyph:null,formConfidence:"medium",note:"The ten sefirot, each with its name of God, its order of angels, its sphere and its intelligence, from Eheia and Kether with the Seraphim and the first mover down to Adonai Melech and Malkuth. It is the frame for the divine names the next chapter puts to work. The transcription of this chapter is damaged in many places, so only its outline is given here; several of the divine names appear in Hebrew letters in the Hebrew hall."},{order:14,name:"III.11 · The divine names",gloss:"Of the Divine names, and their power and vertue",value:"Book III, chapter 11; pp. 370–379 in the English of 1651",numeric:11,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The arts by which names are drawn from scripture — notariqon, gematria (J.F.’s “Gimetrian”), Ziruph, transposition — and the names they yield, Agla and Ararita among them. Such names, after Origen, are to be kept “without corruption in their own Characters”. The chapter prints the ABRACADABRA triangle, reporting Serenus Sammonicus’s precept against fever, and two seals: one from “Rabbai Hama in his book of speculation”, with “the four squared names of God” on its face and the name Ararita on its back, and one made from “the beginnings and ends of the five first verses of Genesis”."},{order:15,name:"III.12 · How the names descend",gloss:"Of the influence of the divine names through all the middle causes into these inferior things",value:"Book III, chapter 12; pp. 379–381 in the English of 1651",numeric:12,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"How the power of the divine names comes down through angels, heavens and stars, and how the virtue of the four-letter name passed to the name of Jesus. It is not a chapter on characters: it has no alphabet, figure or table, and its only seal is the cross, which it calls the seal of that name."},{order:16,name:"III.23 · The tongue of angels",gloss:"Of the tongue of Angels, and of their speaking amongst themselves, and with us",value:"Book III, chapter 23; pp. 412–414 in the English of 1651",numeric:23,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Agrippa reports the opinion that if angels use any language it is Hebrew, “because that was the first of all, and came from heaven” — and then says that “how Angels speak it is hid from us, as they themselves are”. The opinion is reported as many people’s, not laid down as his own."},{order:17,name:"III.24 · Names of spirits",gloss:"Of the names of Spirits, and their various imposition; and of the Spirits that are set over the Stars, Signs, Corners of the Heaven, and the Elements",value:"Book III, chapter 24; pp. 414–417 in the English of 1651",numeric:24,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Lists of names: the angels of the planets, Zaphiel over Saturn to Gabriel over the Moon, with a second set that runs from Oriphiel; the angels of the twelve signs; and the twenty-eight who rule the mansions of the Moon. Here too is Agrippa’s claim to have seen a spirit’s name and seal written on virgin parchment and fed to a water-frog, after which rain followed. The chapter cites the treatise “Abbas Tritemius” wrote to the Emperor Maximilian on the seven governing spirits."},{order:18,name:"III.25 · The seventy-two angels",gloss:"How the Hebrew Mecubals draw forth the sacred names of Angels out of the sacred writ, and of the seventie two Angels, which bear the name of God, with the Tables of Ziruph, and the Commutations of letters, and numbers",value:"Book III, chapter 25; pp. 417–427 in the English of 1651",numeric:25,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The seventy-two angels. Three verses of Exodus of seventy-two letters each, which the 1651 text names by their opening words Vajisa, Vajabo and Vajot (now numbered 14:19–21), are drawn out in lines, the middle verse running the contrary way; the three letters that then stand one under another make each name, and El or Iah added makes the angel’s, from Vehuiah to Mumiah. Six tables of letters follow — commutations, Ziruph and numeral transpositions — among them “The Table of the Combinations of Ziruph”."},{order:19,name:"III.26 · Names from the heavens",gloss:"Of finding out of the names of spirits, and Genius’s from the disposition of Celestiall bodies",value:"Book III, chapter 26; pp. 428–429 in the English of 1651",numeric:26,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Names drawn from the heavens: the twenty-two Hebrew letters are cast round the figure of the heavens degree by degree from the ascendant, and the letters that fall on the chosen stars, read in order, give the name of a good spirit; cast against the order of the signs, of an evil one. Such names, “although unknown in sound”, may do more, Agrippa says, because the mind is astonished at their obscurity."},{order:20,name:"III.27 · Reckoning names",gloss:"Of the calculating Art of such names by the tradition of Cabalists",value:"Book III, chapter 27; pp. 430–434 in the English of 1651",numeric:27,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Calculating tables headed “The entrance of the good Angels” and of the evil, under the seven planets and the twelve signs, with the rule that “every Angelicall name must proceed from some primary name of God”. Agrippa allows the calculation in Chaldean, Arabic, Egyptian, Greek and Latin letters as well as Hebrew, and names an “Alfonsus Cyprius” who fitted it to Latin characters."},{order:21,name:"III.29 · Characters of spirits",gloss:"Of the Characters and Seals of spirits",value:"Book III, chapter 29; pp. 437–438 in the English of 1651",numeric:29,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Characters are “certain unknowable letters and writings”, kept from profane eyes. After Porphyry on sacred figures — a circle for the heaven, parts of a circle for the Moon, the triangle for Juno — Agrippa prints the alphabet “which Peter Apponus notes, as delivered by Honorius of Thebes”, set against the Latin letters A to Z. He never calls it Theban; the later name comes from the man, whom the Latin of 1533 calls Honorius Thebanus."},{order:22,name:"III.30 · The Hebrew alphabets",gloss:"Another manner of making Characters, delivered by Cabalists",value:"Book III, chapter 30; pp. 439–444 in the English of 1651",numeric:30,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The chapter of the Hebrew-keyed alphabets. Agrippa names first an ancient writing of Moses and the prophets, which he does not print, and says the letters in use “at this day” were instituted by Esdras (Ezra). Then come the Celestial writing (p. 439), the Malachim (p. 440) and the Passing of the River (p. 441); the twenty-seven letters in nine chambers, with the name Michael worked down to a single character; and characters made by joining letters, a fashion “most received” among the Arabians. It closes by saying that angels have no characters of their own."},{order:23,name:"III.31 · Revealed signs",gloss:"There is yet another fashion of Characters, and concerning marks of spirits which are received only by revelation",value:"Book III, chapter 31; pp. 445–447 in the English of 1651",numeric:31,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Signs received only by revelation, with three stories Agrippa repeats: the sign shown to Constantine with the words “In hoc vince”; the pentangle revealed to Antiochus Soter, which resolved into letters spells the Greek word for health; and the sign an angel gave to Judas, “who by reason of that was afterward sirnamed Machabeus”. Agrippa tells them as fact; they are reported here as legends he repeats. The figures stand on p. 446 of the English."},{order:24,name:"III.65 · The conclusion",gloss:"The Conclusion of the whole Work",value:"Book III, chapter 65; pp. 555–556 in the English of 1651",numeric:65,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"Agrippa admits that the book is scattered on purpose: some things written in order, some without, some left “for the search of the intelligent”, the truth “folded up” in enigmas so that it is hidden from the wicked and open to the wise. It is to be read with II.22, where the making of the seals is left to “the wise searcher”."},{order:25,name:"The Censure",gloss:"The Censure, or Retractation of Henry Cornelius Agrippa, concerning Magick, after his declamation of the vanity of Sciences, and the excellency of the word of God",value:"After Book III and the letters, before the colophon; pp. 567–583 in the English of 1651",numeric:null,glyph:null,formConfidence:"high",note:"The magic chapters of De vanitate (1530), printed at the end of the book they condemn: on magic in general, natural, mathematical and enchanting magic, goetia, theurgy, the Cabala and juggling. The recantation stands a few sentences from the end of the last of them, on juggling (p. 582), and then comes the colophon, “Anno M. D. XXXIII. In the Moneth of Iuly.” The chapter on the Cabala contains anti-Jewish polemic; it is reported here as Agrippa’s of 1530 and not quoted."}],transmission:[{slug:"draft",title:"The first draft, written for Trithemius",kind:"manuscript",year:1509,end:1510,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,when:"winter 1509–10",description:"After a stay at Trithemius’s abbey in Würzburg and talk together “concerning Chymistry, Magick, and Cabalie”, Agrippa writes the three books and sends them to the abbot for his “correction and censure”. What the draft held of the printed text, and whether it held any of the alphabets, is not known.",dating:"The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy dates the visit to the winter of 1509–10; Trithemius’s reply of 8 April 1510 is the latest date for the draft."},{slug:"trithemius-reply",title:"Trithemius replies",kind:"text",year:1510,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"8 April 1510",description:"The abbot praises the work and gives one rule: to “communicate vulgar secrets to vulgar friends, but higher and secret to higher, and secret friends only” (J.F., 1651). The letter is printed in the front matter of 1533, dated from “our Monastery of Peapolis”."},{slug:"circulation",title:"The draft goes from hand to hand",kind:"manuscript",year:1510,end:1531,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,when:"1510s–1520s",description:"By Agrippa’s account the draft was intercepted before he had finished it and flew abroad “in Italy, in France, in Germany through many mens hands”, and some would have printed it unfinished (“To the Reader”, 1533).",dating:"Agrippa gives no dates; the span runs from the reply of 1510 to the printing."},{slug:"letter-1527",title:"The letter from Lyons",kind:"text",year:1527,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"24 September 1527",description:"Agrippa writes to Aurelius de Aquapendente that the first two books are “dificient in many things” and the third “wholy imperfect”, and promises the whole work, “reserving the key thereof for most intimate friends only”. The pseudo-Agrippan Fourth Book would later present itself as that key. The letter is printed after Book III."},{slug:"de-vanitate",title:"De incertitudine et vanitate scientiarum",kind:"text",year:1530,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Printed at Antwerp by Joannes Grapheus: a declamation against all the arts, magic and the Cabala among them, sceptical of every script’s claim to antiquity, whose chapters on magic end with a recantation of De occulta philosophia. The attacks it drew, Agrippa later wrote, helped to hold back Books II and III."},{slug:"book-one",title:"Book I printed alone",kind:"text",year:1531,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Printed at Antwerp by Joannes Grapheus (February 1531) and at Paris by Wechel, with the dedication to Hermann von Wied, archbishop-elector of Cologne, dated at Mechelen in January 1531. The rest was held back, Agrippa wrote, by “the suddain, and almost unexpected death of holy Margaret of Austria my Princess”.",dating:"The printings are given from library catalogue records. None of the alphabets of Books II and III can be in a printing of Book I. Whether the printing of 1531 already carried the table at the end of I.74, with the Chaldean alphabet that II.19 places “in the end of the first book”, is not known: no copy of 1531 has been examined for this page."},{slug:"complete-1533",title:"The three books complete",kind:"text",year:1533,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"July 1533",description:"The first complete edition, much enlarged, with the dedications of Books II and III to Hermann von Wied, the letters of 1510, “To the Reader”, the later letters after Book III, and the Censure before the colophon “Anno M. D. XXXIII. Mense Iulio.” It holds the earliest printings the wing can document of the Celestial and Malachim alphabets, the nine chambers, and the planetary tables with their names and seals as a set; the Honorian alphabet had been printed by Trithemius in 1518, and the Passing of the River is reported in Abraham de Balmes’s Hebrew grammar Mikneh Avram (Venice, 1523).",dating:"The imprint is unsigned. Catalogues supply Cologne and the printer Johannes Soter in brackets, the Library of Congress and Eton with a query; Eton’s record also notes Adams’s suggestion of Basel. The McGill copy is catalogued as one of several variant printings of the first edition."},{slug:"death",title:"Agrippa dies",kind:"event",year:1535,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Agrippa dies two years after the complete edition appeared; the year is the one the library catalogues give."},{slug:"jf-1651",title:"Three Books of Occult Philosophy in English",kind:"text",year:1651,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, “By J. F.”. It translates the whole volume of 1533 — front matter, three books, letters and Censure, down to the colophon — and adds a Life of Agrippa, an encomium signed “Eugenius Philalethes”, a translator’s preface, errata and a table of contents. It is still the standard English.",dating:"Wing A789. The EEBO-TCP catalogue calls the translator probably John French (1616–1657); the Wellcome catalogue gives “J.F[reake]” and likewise names French as probable."},{slug:"turner-1655",title:"Turner’s “Fourth Book”",kind:"text",year:1655,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Robert Turner’s Henry Cornelius Agrippa his Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy (London: J.C. for John Harrison) puts into English, under Agrippa’s name, the Latin Fourth Book printed after his death, with the Heptameron ascribed to Pietro d’Abano, two geomancies and the Arbatel. Much that later English magic credits to “Agrippa” comes from this volume, not from the three books.",dating:"Wing A785; another issue appeared the same year, and another edition in 1665."},{slug:"vanity-1676",title:"The Vanity of Arts and Sciences",kind:"text",year:1676,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"The English De vanitate (London: J.C. for Samuel Speed). Its second chapter, on the first elements of letters, doubts that any script can prove its first origin; the recantation of the three books stands at the close of chapter XLVIII (p. 128)."},{slug:"barrett",title:"Barrett’s Magus",kind:"text",year:1801,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Francis Barrett’s The Magus, or Celestial Intelligencer (London) compiles Agrippa and the pseudo-Agrippan Fourth Book. Its engraved plate of the Theban, Celestial, Malachim and Passing the River is often said to be the version modern charts copy; that has not been shown."},{slug:"perrone-compagni",title:"The critical edition",kind:"text",year:1992,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Vittoria Perrone Compagni’s De occulta philosophia libri tres (Leiden: Brill; Studies in the History of Christian Thought 48), the scholarly Latin text, which reports the early redaction. Its chapter numbers are the ones this page uses."},{slug:"tyson",title:"Tyson’s annotated English",kind:"text",year:1993,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,description:"Donald Tyson’s annotated edition of J.F.’s translation (St Paul: Llewellyn). It is in copyright, and is not quoted here."}],claims:[{claim:"Agrippa wrote De occulta philosophia as a young man and sent the draft to Trithemius, who answered on 8 April 1510.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Agrippa’s dedicatory letter and Trithemius’s dated reply are printed in the front matter of the 1533 edition, in Latin, and of the English of 1651; “To the Reader” tells of the same gift of the draft.",sources:["Agrippa 1533, front matter (McGill copy)","J.F. 1651, front matter","Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Agrippa”"]},{claim:"The book printed in 1533 is the text of 1510.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Agrippa himself says it is not. In 1527 the third book was “wholy imperfect”, “a certain Epitome”; in January 1531 he had “inserted many things in many places”; and “To the Reader” says “we added some Chapters”. Which chapters can be settled only from the early redaction, which Perrone Compagni’s edition reports.",sources:["J.F. 1651, front matter and letters (pp. 557–559)","Perrone Compagni 1992"]},{claim:"The Honorian, Celestial, Malachim and Passing-the-River alphabets, the planetary seals and the nine chambers go back to the draft of 1510, or to the printing of 1531.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Nothing shows it. The printing of 1531 was Book I alone, and all of them stand in Books II and III; what the draft held is not known. They are dated here to July 1533.",sources:["J.F. 1651, dedication of Book II","Agrippa 1533"]},{claim:"Book I was printed on its own in 1531.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The dedication of Book I is dated at Mechelen in January 1531, and that of Book II speaks of the rest as promised “when I published the first of them”. Library catalogue records give printings at Antwerp (Joannes Grapheus, February 1531) and Paris (Wechel).",sources:["J.F. 1651, front matter and p. 165","Agrippa 1533, McGill copy, dedication of Book I","library catalogue records"]},{claim:"The complete edition was printed at Cologne by Johannes Soter in July 1533.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"The date is secure: the colophon reads “Anno M. D. XXXIII. Mense Iulio.” The place and printer are not signed. McGill catalogues its copy “[Cologne] : [Soter]”, the Library of Congress “[Cologne: s.n. (J. Soter?)]”, and Eton “[J. Soter?]”, noting Adams’s suggestion of Basel.",sources:["McGill Library copy","Library of Congress record","Eton College Library record B25098","J.F. 1651, colophon"]},{claim:"Agrippa recanted De occulta philosophia in his De vanitate of 1530.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The recantation stands at the close of the chapters on magic in De vanitate (chapter XLVIII in the English of 1676) and of the Censure printed with the three books, in Latin in 1533 and in English in 1651; “To the Reader” says he “did for the most part retract this book”. Whether the recantation was sincere, prudent or rhetorical is argued in the scholarship and not settled.",sources:["Agrippa 1533, the Censure","J.F. 1651, pp. 567–583 and front matter","The Vanity of Arts and Sciences (1676), ch. XLVIII"]},{claim:"The three books answer to three worlds: the elemental, the celestial and the intellectual.",standing:"documented",evidence:"It is Agrippa’s own plan, in I.1: a threefold world, “Elementary, Celestiall, and intellectuall”, and of the three books “the first contains naturall Magick, the second Celestiall, and the third Ceremoniall”.",sources:["J.F. 1651, I.1"]},{claim:"Agrippa treats characters at each of the three levels, and puts the alphabets of spirits in Book III.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The arrangement is his: natural characters stamped by the stars in I.33; celestial characters, from geomantic figures and by likeness, in II.51–52, with a pointer from the Malachim, “But of these hereafter”; the characters and seals of spirits, the Hebrew scripts and the revealed signs in III.29–31. To call it a theory in three tiers is this page’s description, not his.",sources:["J.F. 1651, I.33, II.51–52, III.29–31"]},{claim:"Agrippa invented the Celestial, Malachim, Passing the River and Theban alphabets.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"He claims none of them. The Honorian alphabet is one “which Peter Apponus notes, as delivered by Honorius of Thebes” (III.29); the other three are writings found among the Hebrews, and the nine chambers “another fashion amongst the Cabalists” (III.30). Trithemius had printed the Honorian alphabet in 1518, and the Passing of the River is reported in Abraham de Balmes’s Mikneh Avram (Venice, 1523). The modern claim of invention has no support.",sources:["J.F. 1651, III.29–30","Trithemius, Polygraphia (1518)","de Balmes, Mikneh Avram (1523), as reported"]},{claim:"Agrippa taught that characters compel spirits.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Of the angels he says the opposite, in III.30: the marks we consecrate to them are those “by which we cannot any way compel them to us, but by which we rise up to them”. He reports pagan beliefs about compulsion in III.31, after Porphyry, and treats the bonds of spirits in III.33, but his own account of the angels’ marks is not one of compulsion.",sources:["J.F. 1651, III.30, III.31, III.33"]},{claim:"Agrippa shows how the planetary seals are drawn from the magic squares.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"He does not. II.22 leaves the method to “the wise searcher”, and every construction rule published since is a modern reconstruction.",sources:["J.F. 1651, II.22"]},{claim:"Hebrew is the language of the angels.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Agrippa reports the opinion — “many think that if they use any Idiome, it is Hebrew” (III.23) — and then says that how angels speak “is hid from us”. Elsewhere he gives it as the observation of “all wise men” that the Hebrew letters are “the most efficacious of all” (I.74), and, after Origen, has the divine names kept “in their own Characters” (III.11).",sources:["J.F. 1651, I.74, III.11, III.23"]},{claim:"The square Hebrew letters were instituted by Ezra, and Moses used an older script.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"Agrippa states it in III.30, and his De vanitate says the same (chapter 2 in the English of 1676), with other reported origins and the disagreements of the Talmudists. It is a traditional view, given here as his; the history of the scripts is in the Hebrew and Phoenician halls.",sources:["J.F. 1651, III.30","The Vanity of Arts and Sciences (1676), ch. 2"]},{claim:"The “Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy” is by Agrippa.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Johann Weyer, his former famulus, rejected it in his De praestigiis daemonum, and scholarship treats it as spurious; Robert Turner nonetheless printed it in English as Agrippa’s in 1655. It calls itself “a Complement and Key” of the three books, echoing Agrippa’s promise of 1527 to keep “the key thereof for most intimate friends only”.",sources:["Johann Weyer, De praestigiis daemonum","Turner 1655, p. 33","J.F. 1651, pp. 558–559"]},{claim:"“J.F.”, the translator of 1651, was John French.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"The title page gives only the initials. The EEBO-TCP catalogue says “The translator is probably John French”, citing the Dictionary of National Biography (French, 1616–1657); the Wellcome catalogue gives “J.F[reake]” and likewise names French as probable; the National Union Catalog is reported to allow either John French or John Freake.",sources:["EEBO-TCP A26565, catalogue record","Wellcome Collection record"]},{claim:"Trithemius was Agrippa’s teacher.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"The documents show a stay at Würzburg, conversations “concerning Chymistry, Magick, and Cabalie”, a dedication and one reply in which Trithemius praises and advises. “Teacher” or “master” goes beyond them.",sources:["J.F. 1651, front matter"]},{claim:"Chapter III.12 treats letters or characters.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"It does not. It treats the descent of the divine names’ power through angels and stars and the passing of the four-letter name’s virtue to the name of Jesus; its only seal is the cross, called the seal of that name. The chapters on names and characters are III.11 and III.29–31.",sources:["J.F. 1651, III.12"]}],uses:[{use:"A quarry for later charts of alphabets and for grimoires",evidence:"The plate in Barrett’s Magus (1801) engraves the Theban, Celestial, Malachim and Passing the River of III.29–30; that most modern charts follow Barrett is often said, but no survey of the charts shows it. Turner’s English Fourth Book (1655) put pseudo-Agrippan characters into circulation under Agrippa’s name."},{use:"Drawing the names of angels and spirits from scripture and from the heavens",evidence:"III.25 draws the seventy-two names from three verses of Exodus and adds tables of Ziruph and of commutations; III.26 casts the letters round the figure of the heavens; III.27 gives calculating tables to be made “not only by Hebrew letters, but also Chaldean, and Arabick, Aegyptian, Greek, Latine, and any other”."},{use:"Making a character from a name",evidence:"III.30 works the name Michael through the nine chambers into five figures, then three, then one, and shows characters made by joining letters, in Hebrew, Greek and Latin."},{use:"Talismans and amulets, as Agrippa prescribes or reports them",evidence:"II.22: each planet’s table engraved on its metal at a fortunate hour, with the virtues it is said to confer. III.11: the ABRACADABRA triangle, after Serenus Sammonicus, against fever, and Rabbi Hama’s seal, to be made “in most pure gold, or Virgin Parchment”. They are reported as prescriptions, not endorsed."},{use:"Writing the names and seals of spirits, on his own testimony",evidence:"III.24: Agrippa says he saw a man write a spirit’s name and seal on virgin parchment in the hour of the Moon and give it to a water-frog, after which rain followed, and do the like in the hour of Mars with a crow, after which came thunder. The claim is his alone."},{use:"Divining from the numbers of names",evidence:"II.20: the Latin letters numbered from 1 to 900. The sum of a name and the parents’ names, cast out by nine, gives the ruling planet — a method Agrippa ascribes to “Alexandrinus the Philosopher” — and cast out by twelve, a sign."}],quotations:[{text:"I confess whilst I was very yong, I set upon the writing of these books, but hoping that I should set them forth with corrections and enlargements, and for that cause I gave them to Tritemius a Neapolitanian Abbot, formerly a Spanhemensian, a man very industrious after secret things.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), “To the Reader”",note:"“Neapolitanian” is J.F.’s slip: the Latin of 1533 has “abbati Peapolitano”, the abbot of Peapolis — St Jakob at Würzburg."},{text:"Yet this one rule I advise you to observe, that you communicate vulgar secrets to vulgar friends, but higher and secret to higher, and secret friends only.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Johannes Trithemius to Agrippa, 8 April 1510, printed in the front matter of De occulta philosophia (1533)"},{text:"The order and process of all these I shall endeavor to deliver in these three Books: Whereof the first contains naturall Magick, the second Celestiall, and the third Ceremoniall.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), I.1; p. 2 in 1651"},{text:"There are therefore two and twenty Letters, which are the foundation of the world, and of creatures that are, and are named in it, and every saying, and every creature are of them, and by their revolutions receive their Name, being, and Vertue.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), I.74; p. 162 in 1651"},{text:"Now the true Characters of the heavens is the writing of Angels, which amongst the Hebrews is called the writing Malachim, by which all things are described and signified in the Beaven for every knowing man to read.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), II.51; p. 316 in 1651",note:"“Beaven”, for “Heaven”, is the transcription’s reading."},{text:"Now how the seals, and Characters of the Stars, and spirits are drawn from these tables, the wise searcher, and he which shall understand the verifying of these tables, shall easily find out.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), II.22; p. 242 in 1651"},{text:"Characters therefore are nothing else then certain unknowable letters and writings, preserving the secrets of the Gods, and names of spirits from the use and reading of prophane men, which the Ancients called Hyeroglyphicall, or sacred letters, because devoted to the secrets of the Gods only.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), III.29; p. 437 in 1651"},{text:"Of this kind of Character therefore are those which Peter Apponus notes, as delivered by Honorius of Thebes, the figures whereof are such, being related to our Alphabet.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), III.29, introducing the Honorian alphabet; p. 438 in 1651"},{text:"Amongst the Hebrews I finde more fashions of Characters, whereof one is most ancient, viz. an Ancient writing which Moses, and the Prophets used, the form of which is not rashly to be discovered to any; for those letters which they use at this day, were instituted by Esdras.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), III.30, the opening; p. 439 in 1651"},{text:"Now you must know that Angelicall spirits, seeing they are of a pure intellect, and altogether incorporeall, are not marked with any marks or Characters, and pingible figures, or any other humane signs; but we not knowing their essence, or quality, do from their names, or works, or otherwise, according to our fancies devote and consecrate to them figures, and marks, by which we cannot any way compel them to us, but by which we rise up to them;",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), III.30, near the end; p. 444 in 1651"},{text:"Therefore let no man be angry with me, if we have folded up the truth of this science with many Enigmaes, and despersed it in divers places, for we have not hidden it from the wise, but from the wicked and ungodly, and have delivered it in such words which necessarily blind the foolish, and easily may admit the wise to the understanding of them.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia (1533), III.65, The Conclusion of the whole Work; p. 556 in 1651",note:"The closing clause of a longer sentence."},{text:"But I will (God willing) set forth the whole work, being made entire, and revised, reserving the key thereof for most intimate friends only, one whereof you need not at all question but that I reckon you.",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa to Aurelius de Aquapendente, Lyons, 24 September 1527, printed after Book III; pp. 558–559 in 1651"},{text:"But of Magick I wrote whilest I was very yong three large books, which I called Of Occult Philosophy, in which what was then through the curiosity of my youth erroneous, I now being more advised, am willing to have retracted, by this recantation;",language:"English",translation:"J.F. (probably John French), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26565)",source:"Agrippa, “The Censure, or Retractation”, from De vanitate (1530), printed at the end of De occulta philosophia (1533); p. 582 in 1651"},{text:"Verum de magicis scripsi ego iuvenis adhuc, libros tres amplos satis volumine, quos de Occulta philosophia nuncupavi, in quibus quicquid tunc per curiosam adolescentiam erratum est, nunc cautior hac palinodia recantatum volo",language:"Latin",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"But of magic I wrote, while still a young man, three books of ample size, which I named Of Occult Philosophy; whatever in them was then done amiss through the curiosity of youth, I now, grown more cautious, wish recanted by this palinode.",source:"Agrippa, the Censure at the end of De occulta philosophia (July 1533), the same sentence in the Latin",note:"From the McGill copy of 1533, with the spelling normalised. In this sentence what is recanted is “quicquid … erratum est”, whatever in the books was then in error."},{text:"And indeed, such are the alterations hapning through the Vicissitude of times, that there are no Languages or Letters that are able to make good the Antiquity or Truth of their first Original.",language:"English",translation:"Anonymous translator, The Vanity of Arts and Sciences (London: J.C. for Samuel Speed, 1676), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26566)",source:"Agrippa, De incertitudine et vanitate scientiarum (1530), chapter 2, “Of the first Elements of Letters”, its last sentence"},{text:"Therefore in this book, which we have composed and made as it were a Complement and Key of our other books of Occult Philosophy, and of all Magical Operations, we will give unto thee the documents of holy and undefiled verity, and Inexpugnable and Unresistable Magical Discipline, and the most pleasant and delectable experiments of the sacred Deities.",language:"English",translation:"Robert Turner, Henry Cornelius Agrippa his Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy (London: J.C. for John Harrison, 1655), as transcribed by EEBO-TCP (A26562)",source:"Pseudo-Agrippa, the “Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy”, opening section; p. 33 in Turner’s volume",note:"Not by Agrippa: the book that claims to be the key of the three books."}],links:[{to:"theban",relation:"III.29 prints the Honorian alphabet “which Peter Apponus notes, as delivered by Honorius of Thebes”. Agrippa never calls the alphabet Theban: the word belongs to the man, whom the Latin of 1533 calls Honorius Thebanus."},{to:"celestial",relation:"III.30, p. 439 in the English of 1651: a writing called Celestial “because they shew it placed and figured amongst the Stars”. This printing of 1533 is its earliest the wing can document."},{to:"malachim",relation:"III.30, p. 440 in 1651. II.51 calls it “the writing of Angels” and points forward to it."},{to:"transitus-fluvii",relation:"III.30: named on p. 439 of the English of 1651 as “the passing through the River” and printed on p. 441; reported in print ten years before Agrippa printed it."},{to:"aiq-bekar",relation:"III.30: the twenty-seven letters in nine chambers, and the name Michael worked into a character."},{to:"planetary-seals",relation:"II.22: the squares, the divine names, the Intelligences and Spirits and their seals. Agrippa withholds the way the seals are drawn."},{to:"signs-of-the-art",relation:"I.33 (the planets’ letters read in the hand), II.48 (the geomantic figures), II.51 (characters from the geomantic figures), II.52 (signs by likeness, and Hermes’s characters of the Behenian stars)."},{to:"trithemius",relation:"The front matter: Agrippa’s dedication and Trithemius’s reply of 8 April 1510. III.24 cites Trithemius’s treatise to Maximilian on the seven governing spirits."},{to:"charakteres",relation:"III.29 defines characters as “unknowable letters”, which “the Ancients called Hyeroglyphicall, or sacred letters”; III.31 adds Porphyry on signs and images."},{to:"alphabet-of-the-magi",relation:"Absent from Agrippa: the English of 1651 mentions neither Paracelsus nor an alphabet of the Magi, and Turner’s volume of 1655 names Paracelsus only in the Arbatel, among exemplary men, with no alphabet."},{to:"the-books",relation:"The chain of books — Trithemius, de Balmes, Agrippa, the English of 1651, Barrett — through which the alphabets travelled."},{to:"hebrew",relation:"I.74 (twenty-two letters: three mothers, seven doubles, twelve simples), II.19 (the letters as numerals, with the finals), III.11 and III.25 (notariqon, gematria, Ziruph, the seventy-two names), III.23 (Hebrew as the speech of angels, reported), III.30 (the script of Moses and the letters of Ezra)."},{to:"latin",relation:"I.74 gives the Latin letters to planets, signs and elements, and II.20 numbers the Latin alphabet from 1 to 900. Both are Agrippa’s constructions: as the Latin hall notes, Latin itself carries no gematria."},{to:"greek",relation:"I.74 gives the seven Greek vowels to the planets, twelve consonants to the signs and five to the elements and the spirit of the world. II.18 treats the Greek numerals."},{to:"arabic",relation:"III.30: characters made by joining letters, a fashion “most received” among the Arabians. III.26 reports the Arabians’ ways of finding a spirit’s name, and III.27 allows the calculation of such names in Arabic letters."},{to:"pgm",relation:"Late-antique sources Agrippa quotes: Serenus Sammonicus’s ABRACADABRA (III.11), Porphyry on hieroglyphic sacred letters (III.29) and Hecate’s oracle (III.31)."},{to:"phoenician",relation:"De vanitate (1530), chapter 2 of the English of 1676, on the Phoenician letters that Linus is said to have brought into Greece before Cadmus made public “other new Letters of another sort”: Agrippa’s sceptical counterpart to I.74."},{to:"syriac",relation:"De vanitate counts Syriac among the “seven sorts of Letters most famous in Antiquity”."}],cautions:["The wing’s alphabets, the squares and seals and the nine chambers all stand in Books II and III, so they date from the printing of July 1533 — not from the draft of 1510, and not from 1531, when Book I appeared alone. The exception is the table that closes Book I, where II.19 says the Chaldean alphabet is set down; whether that table was already in the printing of 1531 is not known.","Chapters are cited here by book and position (II.22, III.29, III.30), which match the Latin of 1533, Perrone Compagni’s edition and the order of the 1651 volume. The English of 1651 misnumbers three headings in Book II: the fourteenth chapter is headed XIII, the sixteenth XIV and the seventeenth XVI.","Agrippa attributes every one of the alphabets: the Honorian to Peter of Abano’s report of Honorius of Thebes, the Celestial, Malachim and Passing the River to the Hebrews, the nine chambers to the Cabalists. None of them is “Agrippa’s alphabet” in the sense of his invention, and two were printed before him.","The Fourth Book printed under his name after his death is not his. Its characters of good and evil spirits, made of “lines and heads”, and its conjurations should never be shown as Agrippa’s.","The translator of 1651 signs as J.F. He is probably John French, as the EEBO-TCP and Wellcome catalogues say, but the identification is not certain, and the page calls him J.F.","The Censure and De vanitate contain anti-Jewish polemic in their chapters on the Cabala. Of the chapters on magic only the recantation is quoted here, and anything else from them should be read as Agrippa’s polemic of 1530.","The Life of Agrippa prefixed to the English of 1651 says that he was famous “about 1530” and then “composed three Books Of Occult Philosophy”. His own prefaces and the letters of 1510 contradict it.","The 1651 text used here is a transcription that does not reproduce the woodcuts, so no figure of the book is shown on this page. The alphabets, squares and signs have their own pages in the wing, each saying where its forms come from.","The Latin of 1533 quoted here is read from one copy, McGill’s, which is catalogued as one of several variant printings of the first edition; other copies may differ in details."],sources:[{citation:"Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres ([Cologne: J. Soter?], July 1533), Library of Congress copy (BF1598.A3 O4 1533).",url:"https://www.loc.gov/item/20007812"},{citation:"The same, McGill Library copy, digitised at the Internet Archive: front matter, III.29, the Censure and the colophon.",url:"https://archive.org/details/McGillLibrary-osl_henrici-cornelii-agrippae_folioA279o1533-19978"},{citation:"The same, Eton College Library catalogue record B25098 (“[J. Soter?]”; Adams suggests Basel).",url:"https://catalogue.etoncollege.com/B25098"},{citation:"Agrippa, Three Books of Occult Philosophy, trans. J.F. (London: R.W. for Gregory Moule, 1651; Wing A789), EEBO-TCP A26565, keyed text (CC0), Text Creation Partnership repository.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26565"},{citation:"The same, EEBO-TCP A26565 at the University of Michigan.",url:"https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo/A26565.0001.001?view=toc"},{citation:"The same, Wellcome Collection catalogue record (“J.F[reake]”, probably John French).",url:"https://wellcomecollection.org/works/ftahuupv"},{citation:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres, ed. Vittoria Perrone Compagni (Leiden: Brill, 1992; Studies in the History of Christian Thought 48), Wellcome Collection record.",url:"https://preview.wellcomecollection.org/works/ymvvbx6a"},{citation:"Agrippa, The Vanity of Arts and Sciences (London: J.C. for Samuel Speed, 1676; Wing A790), EEBO-TCP A26566: chapter 2 and chapters XLI–XLVIII.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26566"},{citation:"Robert Turner (trans.), Henry Cornelius Agrippa his Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy (London: J.C. for John Harrison, 1655; Wing A785), EEBO-TCP A26562.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26562"},{citation:"The same, another issue of 1655 (Wing A785A), EEBO-TCP A26563, whose transcription includes the Arbatel.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26563"},{citation:"The same, the edition of 1665 (London: J.C. for the Rooks; Wing A786), EEBO-TCP A26564.",url:"https://github.com/textcreationpartnership/A26564"},{citation:"Johannes Trithemius, Polygraphiae libri sex (July 1518), Library of Congress catalogue record: the first printing of the Honorian alphabet.",url:"https://www.loc.gov/item/32017914"},{citation:"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim”.",url:"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/agrippa-nettesheim/"},{citation:"Joseph H. Peterson (ed.), Agrippa, Three Books of Occult Philosophy, Book III, Esoteric Archives.",url:"https://www.esotericarchives.com/agrippa/agripp3c.htm"},{citation:"Donald Tyson (ed.), Three Books of Occult Philosophy (St Paul: Llewellyn, 1993); in copyright, not quoted."},{citation:"David A. King, The Ciphers of the Monks: A Forgotten Number-Notation of the Middle Ages (Stuttgart: Steiner, 2001), on the medieval number-signs whose rule II.19 follows."}]};export{e as default};
